WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.650
SPEAKER 1:
Representative government

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:06.810
requires a society separate
from the government big enough

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:09.030
to control that great power.

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:16.750
So we said in the
Declaration of Independence

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:21.460
that the King was
suspending the legislature

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:25.180
and wouldn't let them
elect another one, right?

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:28.900
Elect another one-- the
representatives of the people

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.030
are not meeting.

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:35.020
You see, consent gives
rise to representation.

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:42.335
Now, why would that make for
a control on the government?

00:00:42.335 --> 00:00:43.710
SPEAKER 2: Because
the government

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:46.770
doesn't receive its
legitimacy without the consent

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:48.960
of the governed.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:49.830
SPEAKER 1: Yeah.

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:54.390
And it isn't even
abstract, right?

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:58.548
Government is a monopoly.

00:00:58.548 --> 00:01:02.010
If it's true, as we said
before, that politics

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:04.110
is natural to the human
being, that they always

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.870
live under laws, then it
arises from their gift

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:09.720
of speech, which is the
classical argument and also

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:12.687
the founders' argument
about government.

00:01:12.687 --> 00:01:14.770
Then there's always going
to be government, right?

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:16.478
And you're going to
be in the government.

00:01:16.478 --> 00:01:19.250
And it's going to have
a monopoly on force.

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:23.020
On the other hand, it
can't really do anything

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:25.450
unless you say it can.

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:27.720
And that's not the king's idea.

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:31.320
The king's idea is you're
going to have to have kings.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.060
It's in the order of nature.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:34.890
I was born to this.

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:37.740
I'm going to be the darn king.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:39.750
And you're going
to be the people,

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:41.340
and I'm supposed to
take care of you,

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:43.920
and you're supposed
to do what I say.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:48.360
And that's not grounded in the
consent of either one of us.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:49.440
We have this nation.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:50.730
We're stuck with it.

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:53.280
Once you change
that and you say,

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:57.600
the government can really only
do what you say it can do,

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:00.840
that limits it drastically.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:02.510
And how is the
government established?

00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:04.260
What's the first step
in the establishment

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:06.090
of the American government?

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:09.747
SPEAKER 3: Breaking away from
the government we were under.

00:02:09.747 --> 00:02:11.039
SPEAKER 1: It happened in the--

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:14.520
yeah, OK, that was good--

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:17.070
we have a code.

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:18.570
It happened in the what?

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:19.728
In the--

00:02:19.728 --> 00:02:21.480
SPEAKER 4: Declaration
of Independence.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:23.272
SPEAKER 1: Yeah, we
all got that one right.

00:02:23.272 --> 00:02:23.814
So--

00:02:23.814 --> 00:02:25.950
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:27.420
--we get the government, right?

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:29.730
Now we've given consent.

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:30.375
Is that enough?

00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:34.450
What else do we need?

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:34.775
SPEAKER 5: Constitution.

00:02:34.775 --> 00:02:35.850
SPEAKER 6: Structure.

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:39.600
SPEAKER 1: Right, because
we have to keep giving it.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:42.630
You have to give consent
to form the government.

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:45.930
But then after that,
do you need more?

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:49.820
Do you need to be saying,
OK, on an ongoing basis?

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:52.640
Because if you do, you're
going to need a constitution.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:55.850
What was Hitler's
constitution like?

00:02:55.850 --> 00:02:58.070
SPEAKER 7: Come and
believe what I say, or die.

00:02:58.070 --> 00:02:59.040
SPEAKER 1: Yeah.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:02.120
He actually-- I don't expect
you kids to read my books,

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:03.400
so I can quote them.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:05.600
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:08.900
Hitler came to power in 1933.

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:12.770
And Hitler wouldn't take
the job unless Hindenburg

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:14.720
and the leaders of the
main political parties

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:18.650
would promise to
support an enabling act,

00:03:18.650 --> 00:03:22.170
he called it, that would
liberate the government

00:03:22.170 --> 00:03:24.930
to do whatever it wanted to do.

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:28.890
So Hitler's constitution
is Hitler's will.

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:32.770
And that'll work if one person
gets to make all the decisions.

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:35.430
But if a lot of people are
going to make the decision,

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:39.060
you're going to have to
have some form by which they

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:45.690
can indicate authoritatively
what they agree to.

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:48.420
So if you're going to
involve a lot of people

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:52.500
in the government, you're going
to have to have a Constitution.

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:54.150
And do you see why
the Constitution

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:58.260
has to be different from
the things that is actually

00:03:58.260 --> 00:03:59.970
in the law?

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:02.250
Because it prescribes
the process by which

00:04:02.250 --> 00:04:04.440
the ordinary laws would be made.

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:07.920
It can't be the
same as that, right?

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:09.900
And that is, by the
way, implied right

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:11.640
through the middle of the
Declaration of Independence

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:14.098
when they talked about the king
messing with the government

00:04:14.098 --> 00:04:17.190
all the time in the way
that he was doing, you see?

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:23.120
So consent requires
representation.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:29.010
And representation requires a
written way for that to happen.

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:33.480
If you have a constitution, you
can spread the decisions out

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:35.665
over time.

00:04:35.665 --> 00:04:37.290
By having representation,
we've already

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:40.897
said you spread the decisions
out over space, right?

00:04:40.897 --> 00:04:42.480
You're going to have
a bigger country,

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:44.670
and everybody can
have a say in it

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:47.010
because they don't have
to go there all the time.

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:53.460
They can all have the same
say in it, but also over time.

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:57.010
Government is by
nature very powerful.

00:04:57.010 --> 00:04:59.640
It has a monopoly on power.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:02.010
It has to be like that.

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:06.870
Representative government
requires a society

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:09.930
separate from the
government, big enough

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:12.660
to control that great power.

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:14.530
SPEAKER 8: Thank
you for watching.

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:16.500
We hope you're enjoying
our Highlights series

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:19.380
and invite you to explore
all of Hillsdale College's

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:20.580
online courses.

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:24.740
They are free and for
everyone who loves to learn.

